@inherits LayoutComponentBase

@*
    The playground's counterpart to the site's Page.astro, and it is the same three boxes in the
    same order for a reason: the header, then a <main> that owns the air above and below, then a
    .shell that owns the measure, then the brand, then whatever the page has to say.

    IT USED TO BE ONE BOX DOING ALL OF IT — a single `<main class="page">` at 60rem with its own
    padding — and one box cannot hold two different numbers. The site sets its header to 60rem
    and its content to 72rem, so a playground that set both to 60rem put every word 103 pixels
    to the right of where the same word sits on /why-justdummies, and its own top padding
    (space-12 against the site's space-8) put them 16 pixels lower. Split the way the site
    splits it, the numbers are the site's numbers and there is nowhere left for them to drift.

    `.page-body` — the 60rem column the site's comparative page sets its own section to, so a
    line of prose breaks in the same place on both halves of the product — used to be the
    fourth box, wrapped around @Body here. IT BELONGS TO THE PROSE, NOT TO THE PAGE, and
    wrapping everything in it was the difference between the playground's code card and the
    landing page's: the site gives its prose 60rem and its figures the whole 72rem shell
    (HomeContent.astro says so where it caps its own subtitle at 56rem and lets the expression
    below it run full width), and a wrapper here could only give both the narrower of the two.
    Each page opens its own `.page-body` around the words that want it now, and what is a
    figure — the playground's card — sits directly in the shell, at the measure the landing
    page's card is drawn at. tests/browser/code-card-parity.spec.ts is what holds the two
    together from here on.
*@

<SiteHeader />

<main>
    <div class="shell">
        <BrandHeading />

        @Body
    </div>
</main>

<SiteFooter />
